<subexercises xml:id="pr-InterpretationToModel">
<title>Part F</title>
<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Write out the model that corresponds to the interpretation given.</p>
<tabular><col halign="right"/><col halign="left"/>
<row><cell>UD:</cell><cell>natural numbers from 10 to 13</cell></row>
<row><cell>Ox:</cell><cell><m>x</m> is odd.</cell></row>
<row><cell>Sx:</cell><cell><m>x</m> is less than 7.</cell></row>
<row><cell>Tx:</cell><cell><m>x</m> is a two-digit number.</cell></row>
<row><cell>Ux:</cell><cell><m>x</m> is thought to be unlucky.</cell></row>
<row><cell>Nxy:</cell><cell><m>x</m> is the next number after <m>y</m>.</cell></row></tabular></statement>
<solution>
<tabular><col halign="right"/><col halign="center"/><col halign="left"/>
<row><cell>UD</cell><cell>=</cell><cell>{10,11,12,13}</cell></row>
<row><cell><m>\extension{O}</m></cell><cell>=</cell><cell>{11,13}</cell></row>
<row><cell><m>\extension{S}</m></cell><cell>=</cell><cell><m>\emptyset</m></cell></row>
<row><cell><m>\extension{T}</m></cell><cell>=</cell><cell>{10,11,12,13}</cell></row>
<row><cell><m>\extension{U}</m></cell><cell>=</cell><cell>{13}</cell></row>
<row><cell><m>\extension{N}</m></cell><cell>=</cell><cell>{<m>\ntuple{11,10}</m>,<m>\ntuple{12,11}</m>,<m>\ntuple{13,12}</m>}</cell></row></tabular></solution></exercise>
</subexercises>
<subexercises xml:id="pr-Contingent">
<title>Part G</title>
<introduction>
<p>Show that each of the following is contingent.</p></introduction>
<exercise>
<statement>
<p><m>Da \eand Db</m></p></statement>
<solution>
<p>The sentence is true in this model:</p>
<tabular><col halign="right"/><col halign="center"/><col halign="left"/>
<row><cell>UD</cell><cell>=</cell><cell>{Stan}</cell></row>
<row><cell><m>\extension{D}</m></cell><cell>=</cell><cell>{Stan}</cell></row>
<row><cell><m>\referent{a}</m></cell><cell>=</cell><cell>Stan</cell></row>
<row><cell><m>\referent{b}</m></cell><cell>=</cell><cell>Stan</cell></row></tabular>
<p>And it is false in this model:</p>
<tabular><col halign="right"/><col halign="center"/><col halign="left"/>
<row><cell>UD</cell><cell>=</cell><cell>{Stan}</cell></row>
<row><cell><m>\extension{D}</m></cell><cell>=</cell><cell><m>\emptyset</m></cell></row>
<row><cell><m>\referent{a}</m></cell><cell>=</cell><cell>Stan</cell></row>
<row><cell><m>\referent{b}</m></cell><cell>=</cell><cell>Stan</cell></row></tabular></solution></exercise>
<exercise>
<statement>
<p><m>\exists x Txh</m></p></statement>
<solution>
<p>The sentence is true in this model:</p>
<tabular><col halign="right"/><col halign="center"/><col halign="left"/>
<row><cell>UD</cell><cell>=</cell><cell>{Stan}</cell></row>
<row><cell><m>\extension{T}</m></cell><cell>=</cell><cell>{<m>\ntuple{Stan, Stan}</m>}</cell></row>
<row><cell><m>\referent{h}</m></cell><cell>=</cell><cell>Stan</cell></row></tabular>
<p>And it is false in this model:</p>
<tabular><col halign="right"/><col halign="center"/><col halign="left"/>
<row><cell>UD</cell><cell>=</cell><cell>{Stan}</cell></row>
<row><cell><m>\extension{T}</m></cell><cell>=</cell><cell><m>\emptyset</m></cell></row>
<row><cell><m>\referent{h}</m></cell><cell>=</cell><cell>Stan</cell></row></tabular></solution></exercise>
<exercise>
<statement>
<p><m>Pm \eand \enot\forall x Px</m></p></statement>
<solution>
<p>The sentence is true in this model:</p>
<tabular><col halign="right"/><col halign="center"/><col halign="left"/>
<row><cell>UD</cell><cell>=</cell><cell>{Stan, Ollie}</cell></row>
<row><cell><m>\extension{P}</m></cell><cell>=</cell><cell>{Stan}</cell></row>
<row><cell><m>\referent{m}</m></cell><cell>=</cell><cell>Stan</cell></row></tabular>
<p>And it is false in this model:</p>
<tabular><col halign="right"/><col halign="center"/><col halign="left"/>
<row><cell>UD</cell><cell>=</cell><cell>{Stan}</cell></row>
<row><cell><m>\extension{P}</m></cell><cell>=</cell><cell><m>\emptyset</m></cell></row>
<row><cell><m>\referent{m}</m></cell><cell>=</cell><cell>Stan</cell></row></tabular></solution></exercise>
<exercise>
<statement>
<p><m>\forall z Jz \eiff \exists y Jy</m></p></statement></exercise>
<exercise>
<statement>
<p><m>\forall x (Wxmn \eor \exists yLxy)</m></p></statement></exercise>
<exercise>
<statement>
<p><m>\exists x (Gx \eif \forall y My)</m></p></statement></exercise>
</subexercises>
<subexercises xml:id="pr-NotEquiv">
<title>Part H</title>
<introduction>
<p>Show that the following pairs of sentences are not logically equivalent.</p></introduction>
<exercise>
<statement>
<p><m>Ja</m>, <m>Ka</m></p></statement>
<solution>
<p>There are many possible correct answers. Here are some:</p>
<p>Making the first sentence true and the second false:</p>
<tabular><col halign="right"/><col halign="center"/><col halign="left"/>
<row><cell>UD</cell><cell>=</cell><cell>{alpha}</cell></row>
<row><cell><m>\extension{J}</m></cell><cell>=</cell><cell>{alpha}</cell></row>
<row><cell><m>\extension{K}</m></cell><cell>=</cell><cell><m>\emptyset</m></cell></row>
<row><cell><m>\referent{a}</m></cell><cell>=</cell><cell>alpha</cell></row></tabular></solution></exercise>
<exercise>
<statement>
<p><m>\exists x Jx</m>, <m>Jm</m></p></statement>
<solution>
<p>There are many possible correct answers. Here are some:</p>
<p>Making the first sentence true and the second false:</p>
<tabular><col halign="right"/><col halign="center"/><col halign="left"/>
<row><cell>UD</cell><cell>=</cell><cell>{alpha, omega}</cell></row>
<row><cell><m>\extension{J}</m></cell><cell>=</cell><cell>{alpha}</cell></row>
<row><cell><m>\referent{m}</m></cell><cell>=</cell><cell>omega</cell></row></tabular></solution></exercise>
<exercise>
<statement>
<p><m>\forall x Rxx</m>, <m>\exists x Rxx</m></p></statement>
<solution>
<p>There are many possible correct answers. Here are some:</p>
<p>Making the first sentence false and the second true:</p>
<tabular><col halign="right"/><col halign="center"/><col halign="left"/>
<row><cell>UD</cell><cell>=</cell><cell>{alpha, omega}</cell></row>
<row><cell><m>\extension{R}</m></cell><cell>=</cell><cell>{<m>\ntuple{alpha, alpha}</m>}</cell></row></tabular></solution></exercise>
<exercise>
<statement>
<p><m>\exists x Px \eif Qc</m>, <m>\exists x (Px \eif Qc)</m></p></statement>
<solution>
<p>There are many possible correct answers. Here are some:</p>
<p>Making the first sentence false and the second true:</p>
<tabular><col halign="right"/><col halign="center"/><col halign="left"/>
<row><cell>UD</cell><cell>=</cell><cell>{alpha, omega}</cell></row>
<row><cell><m>\extension{P}</m></cell><cell>=</cell><cell>{alpha}</cell></row>
<row><cell><m>\extension{Q}</m></cell><cell>=</cell><cell><m>\emptyset</m></cell></row>
<row><cell><m>\referent{c}</m></cell><cell>=</cell><cell>alpha</cell></row></tabular></solution></exercise>
<exercise>
<statement>
<p><m>\forall x(Px \eif \enot Qx)</m>, <m>\exists x(Px \eand \enot Qx)</m></p></statement>
<solution>
<p>There are many possible correct answers. Here are some:</p>
<p>Making the first sentence true and the second false:</p>
<tabular><col halign="right"/><col halign="center"/><col halign="left"/>
<row><cell>UD</cell><cell>=</cell><cell>{iota}</cell></row>
<row><cell><m>\extension{P}</m></cell><cell>=</cell><cell><m>\emptyset</m></cell></row>
<row><cell><m>\extension{Q}</m></cell><cell>=</cell><cell><m>\emptyset</m></cell></row></tabular></solution></exercise>
<exercise>
<statement>
<p><m>\exists x(Px \eand Qx)</m>, <m>\exists x(Px \eif Qx)</m></p></statement>
<solution>
<p>There are many possible correct answers. Here are some:</p>
<p>Making the first sentence false and the second true:</p>
<tabular><col halign="right"/><col halign="center"/><col halign="left"/>
<row><cell>UD</cell><cell>=</cell><cell>{iota}</cell></row>
<row><cell><m>\extension{P}</m></cell><cell>=</cell><cell><m>\emptyset</m></cell></row>
<row><cell><m>\extension{Q}</m></cell><cell>=</cell><cell>{iota}</cell></row></tabular></solution></exercise>
<exercise>
<statement>
<p><m>\forall x(Px\eif Qx)</m>, <m>\forall x(Px \eand Qx)</m></p></statement>
<solution>
<p>There are many possible correct answers. Here are some:</p>
<p>Making the first sentence true and the second false:</p>
<tabular><col halign="right"/><col halign="center"/><col halign="left"/>
<row><cell>UD</cell><cell>=</cell><cell>{iota}</cell></row>
<row><cell><m>\extension{P}</m></cell><cell>=</cell><cell><m>\emptyset</m></cell></row>
<row><cell><m>\extension{Q}</m></cell><cell>=</cell><cell>{iota}</cell></row></tabular></solution></exercise>
<exercise>
<statement>
<p><m>\forall x\exists y Rxy</m>, <m>\exists x\forall y Rxy</m></p></statement>
<solution>
<p>There are many possible correct answers. Here are some:</p>
<p>Making the first sentence true and the second false:</p>
<tabular><col halign="right"/><col halign="center"/><col halign="left"/>
<row><cell>UD</cell><cell>=</cell><cell>{alpha, omega}</cell></row>
<row><cell><m>\extension{R}</m></cell><cell>=</cell><cell>{<m>\ntuple{alpha, omega}</m>, <m>\ntuple{omega, alpha}</m>}</cell></row></tabular></solution></exercise>
<exercise>
<statement>
<p><m>\forall x\exists y Rxy</m>, <m>\forall x\exists y Ryx</m></p></statement>
<solution>
<p>There are many possible correct answers. Here are some:</p>
<p>Making the first sentence false and the second true:</p>
<tabular><col halign="right"/><col halign="center"/><col halign="left"/>
<row><cell>UD</cell><cell>=</cell><cell>{alpha, omega}</cell></row>
<row><cell><m>\extension{R}</m></cell><cell>=</cell><cell>{<m>\ntuple{alpha, alpha}</m>, <m>\ntuple{alpha, omega}</m>}</cell></row></tabular></solution></exercise>
</subexercises>
<subexercises xml:id="ql-models-part-i">
<title>Part I</title>
<introduction>
<p>Show that the following sets of sentences are consistent.</p></introduction>
<exercise>
<statement>
<p>{Ma, <m>\enot</m> Na, Pa, <m>\enot</m> Qa}</p></statement></exercise>
<exercise>
<statement>
<p>{<m>Lee</m>, <m>Lef</m>, <m>\enot Lfe</m>, <m>\enot Lff</m>}</p></statement></exercise>
<exercise>
<statement>
<p>{<m>\enot (Ma \eand \exists x Ax)</m>, <m>Ma \eor Fa</m>, <m>\forall x(Fx \eif Ax)</m>}</p></statement></exercise>
<exercise>
<statement>
<p>{<m>Ma \eor Mb</m>, <m>Ma \eif \forall x \enot Mx</m>}</p></statement></exercise>
<exercise>
<statement>
<p>{<m>\forall y Gy</m>, <m>\forall x (Gx \eif Hx)</m>, <m>\exists y \enot Iy</m>}</p></statement></exercise>
<exercise>
<statement>
<p>{<m>\exists x(Bx \eor Ax)</m>, <m>\forall x \enot Cx</m>, <m>\forall x\bigl[(Ax \eand Bx) \eif Cx\bigr]</m>}</p></statement></exercise>
<exercise>
<statement>
<p>{<m>\exists x Xx</m>, <m>\exists x Yx</m>, <m>\forall x(Xx \eiff \enot Yx)</m>}</p></statement></exercise>
<exercise>
<statement>
<p>{<m>\forall x(Px \eor Qx)</m>, <m>\exists x\enot(Qx \eand Px)</m>}</p></statement></exercise>
<exercise>
<statement>
<p>{<m>\exists z(Nz \eand Ozz)</m>, <m>\forall x\forall y(Oxy \eif Oyx)</m>}</p></statement></exercise>
<exercise>
<statement>
<p>{<m>\enot \exists x \forall y Rxy</m>, <m>\forall x \exists y Rxy</m>}</p></statement></exercise>
</subexercises>
<subexercises xml:id="ql-models-part-j">
<title>Part J</title>
<introduction>
<p>Construct models to show that the following arguments are invalid.</p></introduction>
<exercise>
<statement>
<p><m>\forall x(Ax \eif Bx)</m>, ∴ <m>\exists x Bx</m></p></statement></exercise>
<exercise>
<statement>
<p><m>\forall x(Rx \eif Dx)</m>, <m>\forall x(Rx \eif Fx)</m>, ∴ <m>\exists x(Dx \eand Fx)</m></p></statement></exercise>
<exercise>
<statement>
<p><m>\exists x(Px\eif Qx)</m>, ∴ <m>\exists x Px</m></p></statement></exercise>
<exercise>
<statement>
<p><m>Na \eand Nb \eand Nc</m>, ∴ <m>\forall x Nx</m></p></statement></exercise>
<exercise>
<statement>
<p><m>Rde</m>, <m>\exists x Rxd</m>, ∴ <m>Red</m></p></statement></exercise>
<exercise>
<statement>
<p><m>\exists x(Ex \eand Fx)</m>, <m>\exists x Fx \eif \exists x Gx</m>, ∴ <m>\exists x(Ex \eand Gx)</m></p></statement></exercise>
<exercise>
<statement>
<p><m>\forall x Oxc</m>, <m>\forall x Ocx</m>, ∴ <m>\forall x Oxx</m></p></statement></exercise>
<exercise>
<statement>
<p><m>\exists x(Jx \eand Kx)</m>, <m>\exists x \enot Kx</m>, <m>\exists x \enot Jx</m>, ∴ <m>\exists x(\enot Jx \eand \enot Kx)</m></p></statement></exercise>
<exercise>
<statement>
<p><m>Lab \eif \forall x Lxb</m>, <m>\exists x Lxb</m>, ∴ <m>Lbb</m></p></statement></exercise>
</subexercises>
<subexercises xml:id="pr-SemanticsEssay">
<title>Part K</title>
<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Many logic books define consistency and inconsistency in this way:
“ A set <m>\{\metaA{}_1,\metaA{}_2,\metaA{}_3,\cdots\}</m> is inconsistent if and only if <m>\{\metaA{}_1,\metaA{}_2,\metaA{}_3,\cdots\}\models(\metaB{}\eand\enot\metaB{})</m> for some sentence <m>\metaB{}</m>. A set is consistent if it is not inconsistent.”</p>
<p>Does this definition lead to any different sets being consistent than the definition in <xref ref="slmodels-defining-concepts-in-terms-of-entailment" text="type-global"/>? Explain your answer.</p></statement>
<solution>
<p>No, it would not make any difference. The satisfaction of a formula with one or more free variables depends on what the variable assignment does for those variables. Because a sentence has no free variables, however, its satisfaction does not depend on the variable assignment. So a sentence that is satisfied by <em>some</em> variable assignment is satisfied by <em>every</em> other variable assignment as well.</p></solution></exercise>
<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Our definition of truth says that a sentence <m>\metaA{}</m> is <term>true in</term> <m>\model{M}</m> if and only if some variable assignment satisfies <m>\metaA{}</m> in <m>M</m>. Would it make any difference if we said instead that <m>\metaA{}</m> is <term>true in</term> <m>\model{M}</m> if and only if <em>every</em> variable assignment satisfies <m>\metaA{}</m> in <m>M</m>? Explain your answer.</p></statement></exercise>
</subexercises>
<conclusion>
<note component="genai">
<title>GenAI prompts for further practice</title>
<p>Here are some potential GenAI prompts you might use to generate further practice problems, in case you find that helpful.</p>
<p>
<ul>
<li>Give me a small QL model with a universe of discourse, extensions for some one-place and two-place predicates, and referents for some constants. Give me QL sentences one at a time and ask me whether each is true or false in the model. Check my answer and explain any mistakes.</li>
<li>Give me a small QL model and a variable assignment. Give me open wffs one at a time and ask me whether each is satisfied by that variable assignment. After I answer, explain how the model and variable assignment determine the answer.</li>
<li>Give me a small QL model and an open wff containing one or more free variables. Ask me to provide a variable assignment that satisfies the wff, or explain why no such assignment exists.</li>
<li>Give me QL sentences that are not tautologies. Present them one at a time and ask me to construct a model in which each sentence is false. Check whether my model works.</li>
<li>Give me QL sentences that are not contradictions. Present them one at a time and ask me to construct a model in which each sentence is true. Check whether my model works.</li>
<li>Give me pairs of QL sentences that are not logically equivalent. Ask me to construct a model in which they have different truth values. Do not give me a countermodel until I have attempted one.</li>
<li>Give me invalid QL argument forms. Ask me to construct a model in which all the premises are true and the conclusion is false. After I provide a model, check each premise and the conclusion individually.</li>
<li>Give me a QL model and several variable assignments. Ask me which assignments satisfy a given open wff. Include some examples where changing the assignment of a variable makes no difference because that variable does not occur free in the wff.</li></ul></p></note></conclusion>
</exercises>
</chapter>
